// Overlapping impls are REJECTED, not ranked — RFC-0080 M1.
//
// A generic impl covers a whole type constructor: `impl<T> Show for Option<T>`
// is the impl for `Option<Int64>`, `Option<String>` and every instantiation
// nobody has written yet. A second impl for a *particular* instantiation is
// therefore not a more specific alternative to pick between; it is a second
// answer to a question that already has one.
//
// Rust allows exactly this only behind an unstable feature, and has for years,
// because ranking overlapping impls is a genuinely hard design problem. Vyrn
// does not import it: one impl per (protocol, type constructor), checked when
// the second impl is DECLARED rather than when a call turns out ambiguous —
// which is the only time both impls are in hand to name.
//
// One case the rule catches is NOT an overlap, and the diagnostic is careful not
// to call it one: `impl Show for Option<Int64>` beside `impl Show for
// Option<String>` names two disjoint types that can never both match a receiver.
// They are refused anyway, because dispatch keys on the constructor — so the
// limit is one impl per constructor rather than a coherence rule, and the
// generic form is the answer to both shapes.
//
// This file is an INTENTIONAL compile error. It is meant to FAIL `vyrn check`,
// so the parity harness lists it under EXPECTED_CHECK_FAILURE rather than
// running it. Observe the diagnostic with:
//
//   vyrn check examples/protocol_overlap.vyrn
//
// It names both heads and the line of the one that was already there.

protocol Show {
    fn show(self) -> String
}

impl<T> Show for Option<T> {
    fn show(self) -> String {
        return match self {
            Some(v) => "something",
            None => "nothing",
        }
    }
}

// Rejected: `Option` already has a `Show` impl, above.
impl Show for Option<Int64> {
    fn show(self) -> String { return "an optional number" }
}

fn main() -> Int64 {
    let a: Option<Int64> = Some(7)
    print(a.show())
    return 0
}
